Ques1. What is UTF-8? 

UTF-8 (Unicode Transformation Format – 8-bit) is a character encoding system used to store and represent text in computers. It can represent letters, numbers, symbols, emojis, and characters from many different languages. 

For example, English uses simple 1-byte characters in UTF-8, while characters from some other languages may use 2–4 bytes. 

Example: 

Hello 😊 can all be stored using UTF-8. 

UTF-8 helps computers correctly store and display text from different languages and symbols. 

 

Ques2. Pros and Cons of CSV and XLSX 

CSV (Comma-Separated Values) 

A CSV file stores data in the form of rows and columns, with values usually separated by commas. 

Pros: 

Simple and easy to understand. 

Small file size. 

Opens easily in many programs. 

Easy to create and edit. 

Useful for transferring data between different software. 

Cons: 

Cannot store formulas, charts, images, or formatting properly. 

Does not support multiple worksheets. 

Data types may not always be preserved correctly. 

Large or complicated datasets can become difficult to manage. 

XLSX (Excel Spreadsheet) 

XLSX is the standard file format used by Microsoft Excel to store spreadsheet data. 

Pros: 

Supports multiple worksheets in one file. 

Can store formulas and calculations. 

Supports formatting, charts, tables, images, etc. 

Better for organizing and analyzing complex data. 

Can preserve different data types and formulas. 

Cons: 

Usually larger and more complex than CSV. 

Not as convenient for transferring simple data between different systems. 

Some programs may not support all Excel features. 

Can be slower to process when the file is very large. 

 

Ques3.  Encoding 

Encoding is the process of converting data, especially characters and text, into a format that a computer can store, process, and transmit. 

Computers ultimately work with binary data (0s and 1s). Encoding provides a standard way to represent characters using these values. 

For example, the letter A has a specific numerical representation in common encoding systems. UTF-8 then represents that character using bytes that the computer can store. 

Why is encoding important? 

It allows computers to store text correctly. 

It helps different systems understand the same data. 

It supports different languages and special characters. 

It prevents problems such as displaying �, Ã, or other strange characters when the wrong encoding is used. 

Common types of encoding: 

ASCII – mainly represents basic English characters. 

UTF-8 – supports characters from almost all writing systems and is widely used on the internet. 

